  "claimChanged": [
    {
      "slug": "www-ibge-gov-br",
      "key": "45bab32f2922",
      "criterion": "1.1.1",
      "message": "The text alternative \"imagem banner\" is too generic to describe the image.",
      "reusedFinal": "true_positive",
      "relabel": {
        "auditor": "true_positive",
        "skeptic": "true_positive",
        "final": "true_positive",
        "auditorRationale": "The element is a 55x55 icon (a phone on an orange circle) inside the 'Respondendo' card link in 'Nossos sites'. The link's visible text already gives its purpose ('Respondendo Espaço para o informante do IBGE...'), so the icon is decorative. Its alt is 'imagem banner', a placeholder that says nothing about the image and that a screen reader still announces (the container's reading text starts 'imagem banner Respondendo...'). The same alt is used on every banner on the page. This is documented failure F39 (a non-null alt like 'image' on an image AT should ignore), and F30 (placeholder text used as the alternative). The finding's claim that the alt is too generic to work as a text alternative holds. The patch ('Ícone de celular') is weaker than alt=\"\", but that does not change the label. The live page on 2026-10-09 still has alt=\"imagem banner\".",
        "skepticRationale": "The element is a 55x55 orange phone icon (respondendo.png) with alt=\"imagem banner\". It sits inside the 'Nossos sites' card link to respondendo.ibge.gov.br, which also holds the visible text 'Respondendo' and a description. The snapshot shows no aria-label, aria-hidden or role=presentation, so the alt stays exposed and is part of the link's content. A curl of the live page on 2026-10-09 shows the same markup, and every card in the section uses the same alt. I looked for a reason it could pass and found none. If the icon is decorative next to the link text, the non-empty placeholder alt is WCAG failure F39 (e.g. alt=\"image\" on an image that should be ignored). If it is informative, \"imagem banner\" is placeholder text and not an alternative (F30). Either way 1.1.1 fails, so the claim that the alt is too generic holds. The suggested fix ('Ícone de celular') is weak: alt=\"\" is the better fix, because the link text already says where the link goes. The weak fix does not change the label."
      }
    },
    {
      "slug": "www-mit-edu",
      "key": "f6f18e3a8b04",
      "criterion": "1.1.1",
      "message": "The text alternative \"colorful cell\" is all its link holds, and does not say where the link leads.",
      "reusedFinal": "false_positive",
      "relabel": {
        "auditor": "true_positive",
        "skeptic": "true_positive",
        "final": "true_positive",
        "auditorRationale": "The element is the photo in the 'Healthier aging' spotlight card, an illustration of a cell. It is the only content of its own link, which points to the news.mit.edu story 'unmasking zombie cells in aging tissue'. Its alt, 'colorful cell', describes how the picture looks, but the image is a functional image, a control. 1.1.1 asks a control's name to describe its purpose, and H30 asks an image link's alt to describe the destination. A keyboard or screen-reader user meets 'colorful cell, link' as a separate tab stop, with nothing saying it opens the 'Healthier aging' story. The 'Healthier aging' heading link that follows goes to the same URL but is a separate link, not combined with the image as in H2, so it does not give the image link a name. The live page on 2026-10-09 is unchanged. Some auditors would put the redundant link under 2.4.4 instead, but the finding's claim, that the alt is all the link holds and does not say where it leads, is a fair statement of a 1.1.1 failure for a functional image.",
        "skepticRationale": "The element is the 'Healthier aging' card image (a 3D cell illustration) with alt=\"colorful cell\". It is the only content of a focusable <a target=_blank> that leads to the news.mit.edu story on senescent 'zombie' cells. The snapshot and the live HTML (curl on 2026-10-09, unchanged) show nothing on the link that names it: no aria-label, no title, no aria-hidden, no tabindex=-1. Keyboard and screen-reader users therefore reach it as a link named \"colorful cell\". One reason it might pass is that the h3 'Healthier aging' just below is a second link to the same URL. That does not fix this link's own name: WCAG's H2 (merging adjacent image and text links) was not used, and the heading comes after the image. Another reason is that 1.1.1 has no documented failure for this case. But the 1.1.1 Understanding document lists H30 under Situation C (controls), and its own examples of linked images use alternatives that name the destination, not what the picture shows. So the alt describes the picture but not what the link does, which is the finding's claim. This is also a 2.4.4 issue, but the 1.1.1 claim holds by itself."
      }
    },
    {
      "slug": "www-padocadomani-com-br-contato",
      "key": "0b9261bc02c1",
      "criterion": "2.4.6",
      "message": "The heading \"delivery\" names a different topic than the content under it.",
      "reusedFinal": "false_positive",
      "relabel": {
        "auditor": "false_positive",
        "skeptic": "false_positive",
        "final": "false_positive",
        "auditorRationale": "The element is the h3 'delivery' in the 'padoca joaquim antunes' block of the contact page. It sits between 'nossos horários' (store hours) and 'formas de pagamento'. The content under it is the delivery hours: 'segunda a sexta: 8h às 18h30 / sábado: 8h às 17h30 / domingo: 8h às 15h30'. The other two stores use the same pattern ('salão' then 'delivery'), and the site menu also has a 'delivery' link. So the heading names exactly the topic of its content. 'delivery' is a common loanword in Brazilian Portuguese, so it is not even a language mismatch, and the patch's 'Entrega' would be a style change, not a fix. The packet's ancestor text included the content under the heading, so the model had the context and judged wrongly. The snapshot and the live page (2026-10-09) agree.",
        "skepticRationale": "The element is the h3 'delivery' in the 'padoca joaquim antunes' block of the contact/hours page. Its sibling h3 headings are 'nosso endereço', 'nossos horários', 'formas de pagamento' and 'contato'. The snapshot shows the text right after it: 'segunda a sexta: 8h às 18h30 / sábado: 8h às 17h30 / domingo: 8h às 15h30'. These are the delivery service's hours, and they differ from the shop hours under 'nossos horários' (7h30 to 19h and so on). So the heading names exactly the topic of the content under it. 'Delivery' is the everyday Brazilian Portuguese word for food delivery, so the English spelling does not make it unclear. The patch ('Entrega') suggests the model objected to the language, which is not a 2.4.6 matter anyway. The model had the following content in its context and still judged the topic wrong. 2.4.6 is met."
      }
    }
  ],
